## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to apply the Command pattern with a runtime registry for runtime extensibility, decoupling command invocation from implementation.

## Key Concepts
- Command pattern for encapsulating each request as an object
- Runtime extensibility through dynamic registration
- Isolation and error handling for fault tolerance
- Async execution for non-blocking operations

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  name: string;
  execute(args: unknown): Promise<void>;
}

type CommandFactory = () => Command;

class CommandRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, CommandFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory): void {
    this.map.set(name, factory);
  }

  async execute(name: string, args: unknown): Promise<void> {
    const factory = this.map.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`);
    const command = factory();
    try {
      await command.execute(args);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Command '${name}' failed:`, err);
      process.exit(1);
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new CommandRegistry();
registry.register('init', () => new InitCommand());
registry.register('build', () => new BuildCommand());
registry.register('deploy', () => new DeployCommand());
await registry.execute('build', { target: 'prod' });
```

## Design Pattern
The **Command pattern** is ideal here because it encapsulates each operation as an executable object, enabling delayed execution, queuing, and undo functionality if needed.

## Trade-offs
- **Pros**: Loose coupling, runtime extensibility, easy testing (mock commands)
- **Cons**: Additional indirection, slightly more complex codebase, registry can become a global state hotspot

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a pluggable backend system with runtime extensibility, isolation, and fault tolerance for production-grade telemetry.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern for dynamic exporter discovery
- Strategy/Factory for exporter instantiation
- Lifecycle management: init, emit, shutdown
- Hot-reload safety, fault isolation, backpressure handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal interfaces (TypeScript-like)
export interface Metrics { name: string; value: number; timestamp?: number; }
export interface MetricsExporter {
  init(config?: any): void;
  emit(metrics: Metrics[]): Promise<void>;
  shutdown(): Promise<void>;
}

// Registry and factory
type ExporterFactory = (config?: any) => MetricsExporter;

class MetricsRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, ExporterFactory>();
  private exporters = new Map<string, MetricsExporter>();

  register(name: string, factory: ExporterFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  create(name: string, config?: any): MetricsExporter | null {
    const factory = this.factories.get(name);
    if (!factory) return null;
    const exporter = factory(config);
    this.exporters.set(name, exporter);
    return exporter;
  }

  // Hot-reload: atomic swap with versioning
  replace(name: string, factory: ExporterFactory): boolean {
    const existing = this.factories.get(name);
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
    return !!existing;
  }
}

// Usage example
const registry = new MetricsRegistry();
registry.register('prometheus', cfg => new PrometheusExporter(cfg));
registry.register('datadog', cfg => new DatadogExporter(cfg));
registry.register('otel', cfg => new OpenTelemetryExporter(cfg));

// Runtime creation
const exporter = registry.create('prometheus', { port: 9090 });
await exporter.init();
await exporter.emit([{ name: 'latency', value: 42 }]);
```

## Hot-Reload Safety & Fault Isolation

- **Atomic swaps**: Replace factories with versioned references to avoid partially updated state
- **Per-exporter try-catch**: Isolate failures so one backend doesn't bring down others
- **Circuit breakers**: Temporarily skip failing exporters to reduce noise
- **Graceful shutdown**: Implement shutdown to flush buffers and release resources
- **Backpressure**: Queue or batch emissions when downstream is slow, using bounded buffers

